'Pi versus c2: mean raw tokens 60,790 / 111,467; uncached input 7,987 / 11,322; input cache share 86.37% / 89.53%; median/p90 25.95/46.02s versus 63.88/90.61s; mean calls 10.25 / 20.50. Cohort cost $0.00958806 / $0.01500573. A higher cache share did not make c2 cheaper or faster.','',
'Paired task-cluster bootstrap, 10,000 resamples: c2 minus Pi raw tokens +50,677 [95% CI +31,945, +69,410], uncached input +3,335.5 [+2,822, +3,849], wall time +34.32s [+21.43, +42.75]. These screen differences exceed the sampled noise; four tasks cannot establish general model-family performance. Flash and Pro retain their prior 4/4 c2 screens. Detailed decomposition, port comparisons and cache tables below remain authoritative for their named cohorts.','',
'Cache fix: initial prefix stays 2,513 characters on DeepSeek. Complete advanced argument definitions are appended in the discovery result, while canonical runtime validation, permission checks and execution remain intact. The first implementation only covered registry dispatch; a real daemon probe caught that the direct path omitted the definitions. The correction lives in the discovery tool itself. Final Linux registry suite 74/74 and six-adapter wire test 1/1 pass; preceding focused provider checks 125/125 pass. Source and test-support typechecks and frozen Linux build pass. No full regression suite was repeated for a rejected benchmark candidate.','',
'Wire evidence: five real daemon requests retain the complete prior schema list and message history byte for byte; advanced argument definitions appear in request 2, the advanced exec call succeeds, and TodoWrite appends after the existing tools. Six actual serializers (Flash, Pro, Luna, Sol, Grok, MiniMax) preserve 1,735–1,800 schema bytes for advanced discovery and carry definitions in the appended history. Entire JSON bodies share only 137–163 leading bytes in the serializer fixture because the growing message array occurs before schemas; this is distinct from provider token-prefix caching. These are local transport/serializer checks, not paid cache-latency measurements on six providers.','',
'Pruning: the existing microcompactor already batches at 120,000 eligible live-output characters, capped at 25% of the context window, and reduces to half on a batch. A diagnostic of 48 compact/port panel runs across six models found zero prunable characters under its eligibility rule (outputs at least 6,000 characters; retain newest five and latest read per path). Therefore candidate 10/25/50/75% prune-share thresholds never fire on this panel and cannot be ranked by uncached input plus output. Keep the existing batch boundary and append-only history; a new numerical optimum is not established. No extra history rewrite was introduced.','',
